        "id": "table_of_contents-1",
        "text": "Subsecciones de: Chapter 7: Effective Agentic System Design Techniques\nP\u00e1ginas: 186-211\n\u2022 Technical requirements  (186)\n\u2022 Focused system prompts and instructions for agents  (187)\n  \u2022 Defining objectives  (187)\n  \u2022 Task specifications  (188)\n  \u2022 Contextual awareness  (190)\n\u2022 State spaces and environment modeling  (192)\n  \u2022 State space representation  (192)\n  \u2022 Environment modeling  (194)\n  \u2022 Integration and interaction patterns  (196)\n  \u2022 Monitoring and adaptation  (198)\n\u2022 Agent memory architecture and context management  (198)\n  \u2022 Short-term memory (working memory)  (199)\n  \u2022 Long-term memory (knowledge base)  (200)\n  \u2022 Episodic memory (interaction history)  (201)\n  \u2022 Context management  (202)\n  \u2022 Integration with decision-making  (204)\n\u2022 Sequential and parallel processing in agentic workflows  (205)\n  \u2022 Sequential processing  (205)\n  \u2022 Parallel processing  (206)\n  \u2022 Workflow optimization  (207)\n\u2022 Summary  (209)\n\u2022 Questions  (209)\n\u2022 Answers  (210)\n7\nEffective Agentic System\nDesign Techniques\nIn the previous chapter, we explored the coordinator-worker-delegator ( CWD ) model, a robust\nfoundation for multi-agent system design that emphasizes cooperation and division of labor. We delved\ninto the three distinct roles \u2013 coordinators, workers, and delegators \u2013  and discussed the intricate\ndetails of their interactions and contributions to effective task distribution.\nThis chapter begins by establishing the importance of system prompts and focused instructions as the\nfoundation of agent behavior. It then explores the critical concepts of state space representation and\nenvironment modeling that agents operate within. The chapter proceeds to examine agent memory\narchitectures and context management strategies, essential for maintaining coherent agent behavior\nacross interactions. Finally, it covers advanced workflow patterns, including sequential and parallel\nprocessing approaches for LLM-based agent systems. This chapter is divided into four main sections:\n\u2022I Focused system prompts and instructions for agents\n\u2022I State spaces and environment modeling\n\u2022I Agent memory architecture and context management\n\u2022I Sequential and parallel processing in agentic workflows",
